// Progress of a session's preparation, reported per step as the node works
// through it.
//
// Why this exists: session creation used to be a black box. The node prepared
// the workspace, cloned the repo, resolved the agent runtime and started it all
// inside one call, and reported only a single pass/fail at the end. When a task
// failed to start, neither the operator nor the user could tell *which* step
// broke — "exit code 1" is the same answer for "the repo is unreachable" and
// "the runtime is not installed". These frames make each step observable, so a
// failure names its own phase.
//
// Ordering is the natural execution order (see SessionStage). Every stage that
// runs emits exactly one frame: ok=true on success, ok=false with `error` set on
// failure — after which no further stage frames arrive for that session. Frames
// are advisory: the server persists them for display, and a node that never
// sends them (older build) still works, it just cannot say where it stopped.
